from author import *
from rag_api import lab
lab(26,'injection','Security testing: injection, poisoning and tenant isolation',
'''Prompt injection occurs when untrusted text attempts to redirect model behavior. Indirect injection arrives through retrieved documents, logs or tool results rather than the direct user request. Retrieval poisoning manipulates which evidence is selected; sensitive disclosure exposes data to an unauthorized audience.\n\nThese risks are connected but distinct. A document can be relevant yet malicious, or benign yet forbidden to the current tenant. Instructions to ignore prior rules are observable attack content; the enforceable boundary is application policy, not a promise that the model will ignore them.''',
'''Build synthetic attacks with a case ID, entry point, requested forbidden effect and expected safe behavior. Include cross-tenant document IDs, fake tool instructions, secrets marked as synthetic canaries and oversized inputs. Assert actual tool effects and returned evidence, not merely whether the answer says it refused.\n\nAuthorize before retrieval and tool execution, escape output before display, restrict network destinations and bound model/tool work. Keep adversarial text out of production credentials and services; the lab uses isolated synthetic records.''',
'''A model says it cannot reveal secrets while an earlier tool call already retrieved another tenant's record. A filter removes one injection phrase but an encoded paraphrase bypasses it. The issue is enforcement below the language layer.\n\nInspect the full trace to identify entry point, trust-boundary crossing and effect. Add a regression case for the invariant, then vary phrasing so the fix is not a single-string deny list.''',
'''Gate on zero unauthorized reads/writes and zero canary disclosure. Report false blocks on legitimate queries so security controls do not silently make the assistant unusable.\n\nSDETs should preserve attack provenance, observed effect, defense layer and replay instructions. A passing prompt-level refusal test cannot substitute for permission checks in the service and tool implementations.''',
"def allowed(doc,tenant):return doc['tenant']==tenant\nassert not allowed({'tenant':'other'},'current')",
"def leaks(text,canaries):\n    return [c for c in canaries if c in text]",
"assert leaks('public answer',['SYNTHETIC_SECRET'])==[]\nassert leaks('SYNTHETIC_SECRET',['SYNTHETIC_SECRET'])==['SYNTHETIC_SECRET']","def leaks(text,canaries):\n    return []",
('What is stronger evidence than a refusal sentence?',['No forbidden effect in the trace','A polite tone','A higher temperature'],0,'The trace and tool effects reveal whether the boundary was actually enforced.'),['Prompt injection','Retrieval poisoning','Tenant isolation'])
lab(27,'policies','Guardrails: layered validation and least privilege',
'''Guardrails are controls applied at specific boundaries: input validation, retrieval authorization, tool permissioning, output validation and rate limits. Moderation can classify content risks but cannot replace authentication or domain policy. Least privilege gives each component only the operations and data it requires.\n\nAllowlisting provides a positive set of supported actions. Deny lists can supplement it for known attacks but are brittle as the primary permission mechanism. Human confirmation should approve a concrete action with scope and effect, not a vague intent.''',
'''Validate input size and types, derive identity from authentication, filter evidence before retrieval, restrict tool arguments and validate returned domain objects. Sandbox code candidates with bounded resources and no production credentials. Record policy decisions separately from model decisions.\n\nAn approval record includes action hash, principal, resource, expiry and status. Before execution, verify that the action still matches the approved payload and current permissions. Rate limits and anomaly checks bound abuse and accidental loops.''',
'''A user approves a draft patch and the agent later applies a different generated patch using the same approval flag. A tool allowlist includes a generic shell wrapper, effectively restoring unrestricted execution.\n\nTest action mutation, expired approval, revoked access, oversized input and unknown tools. Review false positives by use case and ensure safe fallback or escalation is available when a guardrail blocks progress.''',
'''The safe assistant can read fixtures and propose changes; actual writes require a matching unexpired action approval. This rule is enforced in application code and tested with denied-call fixtures.\n\nRelease evidence identifies every guardrail's boundary and failure mode. Layered controls reduce reliance on any one classifier and make regressions diagnosable with deterministic assertions.''',
"approved={'hash':'abc','expires':200}\nassert approved['hash']=='abc' and approved['expires']>100",
"def approval_valid(record,action_hash,now):\n    return record.get('status')=='approved' and record.get('hash')==action_hash and record.get('expires',0)>now",
"assert approval_valid({'status':'approved','hash':'x','expires':20},'x',10)\nassert not approval_valid({'status':'approved','hash':'x','expires':20},'y',10)\nassert not approval_valid({'status':'approved','hash':'x','expires':20},'x',20)","def approval_valid(record,action_hash,now):\n    return False",
('What must approval bind to?',['The exact action and current scope','Any later action by the agent','The model name alone'],0,'A broad flag cannot prove that a changed side effect was approved.'),['Guardrail','Least privilege','Approval expiry'])
lab(28,'generation','AI-assisted tests: GraphQL contracts and requirement traceability',
'''Generated tests are candidates that require independent validation. For GraphQL, inspect operations, arguments, required fields, nullability, enum values and authorization rules. A syntactically valid query may fail business rules; an HTTP 200 response may contain GraphQL errors.\n\nRequirement traceability connects each candidate to the rule it tests. Count unique covered behaviors, not the number of generated files. Positive, negative, boundary, authentication and authorization cases should remain distinct.''',
'''Parse the schema and operation AST rather than splitting schema text with fragile string rules. Build a coverage matrix for required arguments, invalid enums, nullable/non-null fields, malformed operations and permission rules. Generate structured case definitions with expected data/errors.\n\nFor REST, use OpenAPI contracts and domain examples. For locator suggestions, validate candidates against the DOM and require uniqueness and stability. Regression selection uses dependency edges and historical risk; it must not exclude mandatory critical tests.''',
'''A generated GraphQL test asserts only status 200 and passes when errors contains an authorization failure. A locator suggestion uses a dynamic index that happens to work once. Duplicate paraphrased cases inflate coverage statistics.\n\nCompile/type-check candidates, execute against synthetic fixtures, inspect assertions and retain requirement IDs. Mutate a required field or expected value to prove the test can detect the intended failure.''',
'''The local GraphQL capstone creates candidate cases, validates them and exports pytest-compatible definitions. Its report shows requirement coverage, duplicates, execution failures and rejected unsupported operations.\n\nAn SDET owns acceptance: generated text becomes useful automation only after executable assertions demonstrate the target behavior. Never treat a model's self-reported completeness as test coverage evidence.''',
"response={'data':None,'errors':[{'message':'Unauthorized'}]}\nassert bool(response.get('errors'))",
"def graphql_ok(response):\n    return isinstance(response,dict) and not response.get('errors') and response.get('data') is not None",
"assert graphql_ok({'data':{'item':1}})\nassert not graphql_ok({'data':None,'errors':[{'message':'bad'}]})\nassert not graphql_ok({'data':{'item':1},'errors':[{'message':'partial'}]})","def graphql_ok(response):\n    return False",
('Is HTTP 200 sufficient for GraphQL success?',['No: inspect data and errors','Yes: transport status is the contract','Only for mutations'],0,'GraphQL execution errors can be returned in a successful HTTP response.'),['GraphQL','Traceability','Candidate test'])
lab(29,'verification','Verify generated code: independent oracles and mutation checks',
'''Generated code can import nonexistent APIs, mishandle edge cases or encode the wrong requirement. A compile/type check establishes basic structural compatibility, not correctness. Unit tests check isolated behavior; integration tests check real contracts; static and dependency checks address additional risks.\n\nThe model that wrote a function should not be the only authority judging it. Use independent requirements, trusted fixtures and known counterexamples as test oracles.''',
'''Run format/lint/type checks, targeted unit tests and integration tests in a sandbox before review. Inspect dependencies and API versions against official documentation. Keep secrets and production services unavailable to candidate code.\n\nMutation testing changes a relevant operator or return value and checks whether the suite fails. A surviving mutation reveals a possible assertion gap, though equivalent mutations need review. For a generated classifier, mutate category mapping and UNKNOWN handling.''',
'''A generated test copies the same faulty formula as the implementation, so both agree. Another test asserts only that a function returns something. A hallucinated library import fails only after deployment because no import smoke test ran.\n\nUse boundary values, negative cases and independently computed expectations. Review failing mutations by requirement, and ensure the suite catches permission bypasses as well as numerical errors.''',
'''Candidate acceptance includes executable results, rejected mutations, reviewed dependencies and a human explanation of tradeoffs. Keep generated provenance for audit but evaluate quality by the same standards as handwritten code.\n\nSDETs should distinguish a generator's confidence from test evidence. Code is accepted when independent checks demonstrate the specified behavior, not when a model claims it is production ready.''',
"def allowed(role):return role=='admin'\nassert allowed('admin') and not allowed('viewer')",
"def catches_mutant(test_inputs,expected,mutant):\n    if len(test_inputs)!=len(expected):raise ValueError('length')\n    return any(mutant(x)!=y for x,y in zip(test_inputs,expected))",
"assert catches_mutant([1,2],[2,4],lambda x:x+1)\nassert not catches_mutant([1],[2],lambda x:x+1)","def catches_mutant(test_inputs,expected,mutant):\n    return False",
('What does a surviving relevant mutation suggest?',['An assertion or input coverage gap','The code is certainly correct','The model needs a longer answer'],0,'The suite did not distinguish the changed behavior; investigate whether it matters to a requirement.'),['Test oracle','Mutation testing','Static analysis'])
lab(30,'service','FastAPI: typed boundaries, dependencies and service tests',
'''An AI service should expose typed domain contracts rather than raw provider messages. FastAPI routes accept request models, call application services and return response models. Dependency injection supplies authenticated principals and adapters. Health reports process liveness; readiness reports whether required dependencies can serve requests.\n\nAsynchronous routes help with I/O, but blocking CPU work still occupies execution resources. Offload training and long evaluations to bounded workers or jobs rather than running them on the request event loop.''',
'''The shared service exposes /health, /chat, /classify-failure, /search-failures, /generate-tests and /evaluate. Requests forbid unexpected fields, cap input sizes and carry request IDs. Responses include mode, evidence, status and trace references.\n\nUse dependency overrides for fixture adapters in tests, and exercise real HTTP serialization through TestClient or ASGI transport. Map invalid input to 422, denied access to 403 and unavailable dependencies to a controlled 503. Never return raw exception strings containing secrets.''',
'''A route returns any dict and accidentally includes provider credentials in an error response. Another declares async while calling a blocking network client. A health check makes a paid model request and becomes both slow and unreliable.\n\nTest response schemas, extra fields, auth, dependency failure, timeout and cancellation. Verify OpenAPI matches actual responses and that a readiness failure does not imply process death.''',
'''Run integration cases against all domain endpoints using synthetic data. Measure contract behavior separately from model accuracy and record adapter mode. Deployment evidence includes typed OpenAPI, bounded work, redacted errors and startup checks.\n\nFor SDETs, the AI boundary is familiar API testing with additional uncertainty inside the adapter. Stable service contracts should remain deterministic even when model wording varies.''',
"from fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel,ConfigDict\nclass Request(BaseModel):\n    model_config=ConfigDict(extra='forbid')\n    failure_id:str\napp=FastAPI()\n@app.get('/health')\ndef health():return {'status':'ok'}",
"def http_status(kind):\n    return {'invalid':422,'denied':403,'unavailable':503,'ok':200}[kind]",
"assert http_status('invalid')==422\nassert http_status('denied')==403\nassert http_status('unavailable')==503","def http_status(kind):\n    return 200",
('What belongs in readiness rather than liveness?',['Required dependency availability','A paid generation on every probe','User quality scores'],0,'Liveness detects process health; readiness indicates ability to serve its required workload.'),['FastAPI','Dependency injection','Readiness'])
lab(31,'resilience','Production architecture: queues, cache and idempotent effects',
'''A production AI path includes authentication, admission control, retrieval, provider work, validation and response delivery. Caches reduce repeated work, queues decouple long tasks, circuit breakers limit calls to failing dependencies and fallbacks preserve a smaller safe capability. Each mechanism changes failure behavior and needs a contract.\n\nIdempotency means repeating a request has the same intended effect as executing it once. A timeout is ambiguous: the remote system may have completed work even if the client received no response.''',
'''Key caches by tenant, permission scope, normalized request, prompt/model versions and index generation. Bound TTL and invalidate after permission or document changes. For side effects, persist an idempotency record before execution and return its existing result on duplicate requests.\n\nA circuit breaker moves closed to open after configured failures, then half-open for limited probes after cooldown. Queued work has an expiry and cancellation path. Fallback answers must indicate reduced capability and retain the same authorization rules.''',
'''An open breaker routes to a backup provider with a different schema and the application treats arbitrary text as valid. A global cache returns another tenant's answer. A retry storm fills the queue faster than workers can drain it.\n\nInject provider outage, slow storage, duplicate requests and permission revocation. Check bounded queue growth, consistent duplicate results, visible degraded mode and eventual recovery.''',
'''Architecture acceptance measures successful tasks under failure, not only normal throughput. Document which dependency failures cause abstention, fallback or rejection.\n\nSDETs should verify each transition with controlled faults and preserve traces for diagnosis. A retry mechanism is reliable only when it respects effect safety, capacity and deadlines.''',
"cache_key=('tenant-a','permissions-v2','prompt-v3','index-v4','question')\nassert len(cache_key)==5",
"def cache_key(tenant,scope,prompt,model,index,query):\n    return (tenant,scope,prompt,model,index,query.strip())",
"assert cache_key('a','s','p','m','i',' x ')!=cache_key('b','s','p','m','i','x')\nassert cache_key('a','s','p','m','i',' x ')[-1]=='x'","def cache_key(tenant,scope,prompt,model,index,query):\n    return ()",
('Why include permission scope in a cache key?',['To prevent reuse across different access rights','To increase answer creativity','To avoid response validation'],0,'The cached evidence and answer are authorized only within their original scope.'),['Circuit breaker','Cache invalidation','Queue'])
lab(32,'containers','Docker: reproducible images, non-root runtime and Compose',
'''An image packages filesystem layers and runtime metadata; a container is an isolated running instance. Dockerfile layer order affects dependency cache reuse. Copying dependency manifests before application source lets source-only changes avoid reinstalling dependencies.\n\nContainers improve reproducibility but do not automatically provide secure isolation, persistent data or correct service discovery. Secrets baked into layers can remain recoverable even if deleted later.''',
'''Use a pinned base image policy, locked dependencies, a non-root user and a narrow build context. Set an explicit working directory, install dependencies before copying source and launch uvicorn on the container interface. Provide health/readiness endpoints and graceful shutdown.\n\nCompose connects the API and optional Qdrant on an internal network with named persistent volumes. Keep provider secrets in runtime environment or secret mounts; exclude .env, caches and credentials with .dockerignore. Multi-stage builds can remove compilers from the final runtime.''',
'''The service listens on 127.0.0.1 inside the container and is unreachable through a published port. A vector collection disappears after restart because no volume was attached. An image contains a copied .env.\n\nTest build context, non-root identity, port reachability, health checks, volume persistence and stop behavior. Scan the image and verify dependency versions against the lockfile.''',
'''Deployment evidence includes the image build, API smoke tests and persistent-index restart test when that backend is enabled. Document fixture-only and live-provider modes explicitly.\n\nFor an SDET, container tests are environment contract tests: the same synthetic request must behave consistently in the virtual environment and container, with configuration supplied externally.''',
"# Dockerfile\nFROM python:3.11-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\nCOPY . .\nUSER 10001\nCMD [\"uvicorn\",\"app:app\",\"--host\",\"0.0.0.0\",\"--port\",\"8000\"]",
"def safe_context(paths):\n    return [p for p in paths if not any(part in {'.env','.venv','.git'} for part in p.split('/'))]",
"assert safe_context(['app.py','.env','.venv/x','data/x'])==['app.py','data/x']","def safe_context(paths):\n    return []",
('Where should provider secrets enter the container?',['At runtime through controlled configuration','In a copied .env layer','In the Dockerfile command'],0,'Build layers and images should remain free of credentials.'),['Docker image','Container','Volume'])
lab(33,'ci','CI/CD: ordered checks, artifacts and release gates',
'''Continuous integration validates changes before they become release candidates. A useful pipeline installs locked dependencies, checks content and contracts, runs unit/integration tests, builds the app and executes representative browser and service tests. Continuous delivery packages a candidate after those gates; deployment publishes an approved artifact.\n\nAn AI quality report belongs in the gate sequence. Producing HTML without using its exit verdict does not protect a release.''',
'''Use GitHub Actions jobs with explicit permissions, dependency caches and immutable artifacts. Run deterministic checks on every pull request. Live provider evaluations, when authorized and configured, use bounded datasets and protected secrets; forks must not receive those secrets.\n\nUpload failure traces and evaluation reports using always() while keeping the job failed. Build GitHub Pages with the repository base path and test route reloads before publishing. Link the artifact to code, dataset and prompt revisions.''',
'''A workflow appends || true to the evaluator and deploys despite hard-gate failures. A deployment rebuilds with different dependencies instead of publishing the tested artifact. A path filter skips tests when course JSON changes.\n\nTest that deliberate content corruption and failing cases produce nonzero checks. Review workflow permissions and ensure deployment depends on the complete validation job.''',
'''The course integrity script is a required gate for missing phases, broken lesson references, absent checkpoints and invalid exercise schemas. Local evaluation returns a failing exit code for missing cases or violated invariants.\n\nSDETs should demonstrate a red build from a known regression, then a green build after the fix. This proves that checks are connected to release behavior, not only documented.''',
"# CI command\npython -m pytest labs\npython labs/evals/runner.py --cases data/evaluation.json\nnpm run lint\nnpm test\nnpm run build",
"def deployable(checks):\n    required={'content','unit','integration','build','evaluation'}\n    return required<=set(checks) and all(checks[k]=='passed' for k in required)",
"assert deployable(dict.fromkeys(['content','unit','integration','build','evaluation'],'passed'))\nassert not deployable({'build':'passed'})","def deployable(checks):\n    return False",
('Why deploy the tested artifact rather than rebuild it?',['To preserve the validated inputs and output','To avoid all future tests','To make the bundle larger'],0,'A new build can differ from the candidate that passed the gates.'),['CI','Artifact','Release gate'])
lab(34,'tracing','Observability: spans, redaction and experiment identity',
'''A trace links operations within one task; spans describe timed units such as retrieval, provider calls and tools. Logs record events, metrics summarize distributions and user feedback contributes product outcomes. Correlation IDs connect these views without requiring sensitive text in every record.\n\nAI observability adds prompt/model versions, retrieval IDs, token usage, cost, finish reasons and validation outcomes. Raw prompts and responses may contain private data, so visibility needs access controls, redaction and retention.''',
'''Use OpenTelemetry spans around domain stages and propagate context into HTTP/tool calls. Record safe attributes such as task ID, model ID, mode, duration and counts. Store sensitive evidence separately under controlled access, referencing it by ID.\n\nMLflow, LangSmith or Phoenix can organize traces and experiments; keep portable run JSON as a baseline. Attach dataset/prompt/index revisions so a quality change can be correlated with a release.''',
'''A dashboard shows average latency but cannot distinguish queue delay from model delay. A trace logs Authorization headers or synthetic canaries. An evaluator failure appears as a successful model span.\n\nTest attribute redaction, parent-child span relationships, error status and correlation across retries. Track p95 and failure slices, not only global means.''',
'''During a simulated outage, the trace should identify the failing dependency, retry attempts and final fallback without exposing credentials. Quality reports should link to the corresponding safe trace ID.\n\nSDETs can assert observability contracts as part of integration tests: required spans exist, status matches the outcome and prohibited attributes never appear.''',
"from opentelemetry import trace\ntracer=trace.get_tracer('ci-quality')\nwith tracer.start_as_current_span('retrieve') as span:\n    span.set_attribute('retrieval.index_version','v1')",
"def redact(record):\n    blocked={'authorization','api_key','password','token'}\n    return {k:('[REDACTED]' if k.lower() in blocked else v) for k,v in record.items()}",
"assert redact({'Authorization':'secret','task':'F1'})=={'Authorization':'[REDACTED]','task':'F1'}","def redact(record):\n    return record",
('Which attribute helps attribute a retrieval regression?',['Index generation/version','User password','A random decorative label'],0,'Versioned retrieval artifacts connect observed behavior to changed inputs.'),['Trace','Span','OpenTelemetry'])
lab(35,'economics','Cost engineering: useful-task budgets and routing',
'''AI cost includes input/output tokens, embedding/indexing work, retries, agent steps, evaluation and human review. Price per token is only one input to cost per accepted task. Smaller models, shorter context and caching help only if they preserve the required outcome.\n\nA routing policy sends simple cases to a low-cost path and escalates difficult or uncertain cases. Routing must be evaluated for false confidence, not merely average savings.''',
'''Set per-task and monthly budgets using dated provider rates. Track measured usage rather than estimating solely from characters. Cache authorized deterministic work and stable retrieval results, batch offline embedding jobs and reduce duplicate context.\n\nUse confidence thresholds calibrated on held-out cases to route accepted classifications, uncertain cases to stronger analysis and unsupported cases to humans. Bound agent steps and judge calls as part of the same budget.''',
'''A cheap classifier confidently mislabels product defects as automation issues, lowering billed cost while increasing operational risk. An optimization removes the evidence needed for correct answers. A cache saves tokens but serves stale permissions.\n\nCompare accepted accuracy, abstention, review labor and total spend before and after optimization. Include cold-start/indexing costs and failed attempts in the report.''',
'''Produce a cost-quality-latency comparison for baseline, cached, routed and shortened-context variants. Reject candidates that violate hard safety or quality constraints even if they are cheaper.\n\nSDETs should validate budget enforcement with synthetic high-usage responses and ensure over-budget tasks terminate or degrade visibly rather than continuing hidden work.''',
"attempt_costs=[.003,.004,.002]\naccepted=2\ncost_per_accepted=sum(attempt_costs)/accepted",
"def cost_per_success(costs,successes):\n    if successes<=0:return None\n    if any(c<0 for c in costs):raise ValueError('cost')\n    return sum(costs)/successes",
"assert cost_per_success([2,4],2)==3\nassert cost_per_success([2],0) is None","def cost_per_success(costs,successes):\n    return 0",
('Which optimization must be evaluated for false confidence?',['Model routing by confidence','Renaming a log field','Changing a heading'],0,'Routing can save money by accepting wrong low-cost results unless confidence is calibrated.'),['Cost per task','Routing','Budget'],demo='cost')
lab(36,'load','Performance tests: TTFT, tails and realistic workloads',
'''TTFT measures time to the first generated token; end-to-end latency measures completion; tokens per second describes generation rate. p50, p95 and p99 summarize latency distributions. Throughput counts completed tasks per unit time, while concurrency counts in-flight tasks. These quantities answer different capacity questions.\n\nLoad tests need representative prompt length, retrieval work, output length, cache state and provider limits. Short fixture responses test service mechanics, not live-model generation capacity.''',
'''Use k6 or a controlled client with bounded arrivals, correlation IDs and separate latency measurements. Include cold and warm runs, slow streaming clients, rate limits and long context. Report offered load, accepted load, error rate and completed throughput.\n\nAvoid coordinated omission: a client that waits for each slow response before sending the next request hides overload. Set acceptance thresholds before testing and retain raw samples for tail analysis.''',
'''Average latency looks acceptable while a small group waits thirty seconds. A benchmark excludes failed requests from percentiles and claims a faster system after an outage. A fixture is advertised as provider performance.\n\nTest percentile implementation, record timeout outcomes explicitly and compare the same workload/configuration. Identify whether saturation occurs in queueing, retrieval, network or generation.''',
'''Produce a load report with p50/p95/p99, TTFT, success rate, throughput and mode. A release must meet both latency and quality contracts under the specified load.\n\nFor SDETs, capacity assertions should be reproducible within a documented environment. External provider variability needs repetitions and clear limitations; deterministic service limits can be tested directly.''',
"import math\ndef percentile(values,p):\n    v=sorted(values)\n    return v[max(0,math.ceil(p/100*len(v))-1)]",
"def percentile(values,p):\n    import math\n    if not values or not 0<p<=100:raise ValueError('bounds')\n    return sorted(values)[math.ceil(p/100*len(values))-1]",
"assert percentile([1,2,3,4,100],95)==100\nassert percentile([1,2,3,4,100],50)==3\ntry: percentile([],95); assert False\nexcept ValueError: pass","def percentile(values,p):\n    return 0",
('Why report failed requests alongside percentiles?',['Otherwise dropping failures can make outages look faster','Failures have no user impact','Percentiles imply success'],0,'Latency over only successful requests can hide rejected or timed-out work.'),['p95','Throughput','Coordinated omission'])
lab(37,'faults','Reliability: controlled faults and graceful degradation',
'''Reliability is the ability to deliver an acceptable outcome despite dependency and input failures. AI failures include provider outage, rate limit, timeout, malformed result, hallucinated evidence, stale index, vector-store outage, broken tools, looping agents and context overflow. Not all failures should be retried.\n\nGraceful degradation preserves a smaller truthful capability, such as displaying retrieved evidence without a generated explanation. It must be visible to the user and obey the same permissions.''',
'''Define a failure matrix with detection, retryability, fallback, user response and recovery evidence. Use deadlines, bounded retries, circuit breakers, index freshness checks, schema validation and step budgets. Record final status as successful, degraded, rejected or failed.\n\nInject one failure at a time in deterministic integration tests, then combine realistic correlated failures such as provider rate limits plus queue pressure. Ensure cancelled tasks release resources and duplicate requests cannot duplicate writes.''',
'''A fallback invents an explanation when retrieval is unavailable. A timeout handler retries indefinitely because each attempt resets the total deadline. A stale index passes health checks but returns deleted evidence.\n\nAssert visible degraded status, no unsupported claims, bounded attempts and eventual recovery. Simulate both empty evidence and contradictory evidence, because hallucination defenses depend on more than transport errors.''',
'''A reliability report shows each injected fault, observed status, trace and invariant outcome. Error budgets count user-visible failures over a specified window, with separate safety invariants that cannot be averaged away.\n\nSDETs should make fault cases executable release checks, not a list of possible risks. The system passes when it behaves predictably under the failures it claims to tolerate.''',
"fallback={'status':'degraded','answer':None,'evidence_ids':['D1']}\nassert fallback['answer'] is None",
"def recovery(fault):\n    return {'rate_limit':'bounded_retry','retrieval_outage':'evidence_unavailable','invalid_schema':'reject','loop':'stop','unknown':'escalate'}.get(fault,'escalate')",
"assert recovery('loop')=='stop'\nassert recovery('retrieval_outage')=='evidence_unavailable'\nassert recovery('new_fault')=='escalate'","def recovery(fault):\n    return 'retry'",
('What makes a fallback acceptable?',['Visible reduced capability without invented evidence','Always returning fluent prose','Resetting every budget'],0,'Degradation must remain truthful, authorized and bounded.'),['Graceful degradation','Error budget','Recovery'])
lab(38,'versions','MLOps and LLMOps: versioned inputs and rollback',
'''A reproducible AI release links code, dataset, model, tokenizer, prompt, retrieval index, evaluation rubric and environment. MLOps manages training/serving artifacts; LLMOps adds prompt and provider configuration, retrieval and semantic evaluation. Both need experiment tracking and operational monitoring.\n\nA model alias or file named latest is not sufficient provenance. Use immutable revisions and artifact hashes where available, and record provider aliases with observed dates when exact internals are not exposed.''',
'''Write a release manifest with all version identities, evaluation run ID, deployment image and rollback target. Register accepted artifacts after gates pass. Track experiments in MLflow or portable JSON with parameters, metrics and artifact locations.\n\nMonitor drift in inputs, accepted accuracy, abstention, latency and cost. Canary a candidate against representative traffic under permissions, then promote only after observing gates. Rollback must restore compatible prompt/model/index/schema versions together.''',
'''A prompt rollback leaves the new incompatible index in place. A training dataset includes future incident resolution fields, causing leakage. A provider alias changes behavior while dashboards show no code deployment.\n\nTest manifest completeness, artifact compatibility, reproducible fixture runs and rollback smoke tests. Preserve old indexes and configuration long enough to perform a real rollback.''',
'''The shared platform's release artifact contains dataset version, code identity, adapter mode, prompt/index versions and all evaluation results. A reader can rerun deterministic fixtures from that record.\n\nSDETs verify that deployment uses the gated artifact and rollback restores the previous contract. Operational drift checks complement pre-release tests because external models and data can change after deployment.''',
"manifest={'dataset':'ci-v1','prompt':'triage-v2','index':'docs-v1','model':'configured','evaluation':'run-001'}\nassert all(manifest.values())",
"def compatible(a,b):\n    keys=('schema','embedding_model','dimension','tokenizer')\n    return all(k in a and k in b and a[k]==b[k] for k in keys)",
"a={'schema':1,'embedding_model':'m','dimension':3,'tokenizer':'t'}\nassert compatible(a,a)\nassert not compatible(a,{**a,'dimension':4})","def compatible(a,b):\n    return True",
('What must rollback restore when contracts are coupled?',['A compatible set of model, prompt, index and schema','Only a dashboard label','Only the latest prompt text'],0,'Individually valid artifacts can fail when combined with incompatible revisions.'),['MLOps','LLMOps','Release manifest'])
lab(39,'adaptation','Fine-tuning: data splits, LoRA and independent evaluation',
'''Prompting changes instructions, RAG supplies changing evidence, and fine-tuning changes model parameters for repeated task behavior. Fine-tuning is not a reliable way to maintain rapidly changing facts or enforce authorization. Start with a baseline and a measured failure pattern before paying training and maintenance costs.\n\nSupervised fine-tuning uses input/target examples. LoRA learns low-rank update matrices while keeping base weights frozen; PEFT is a broader family of parameter-efficient methods. These reduce trainable parameters, not the need for careful data and evaluation.''',
'''Create reviewed training examples with UNKNOWN and difficult classes, group related incidents to prevent leakage and reserve a held-out evaluation set. The optional local lab trains a tiny text classifier with torch; the PEFT example uses a configured public base checkpoint and LoRA adapter with explicit download/cost requirements.\n\nRecord base/tokenizer revisions, training hyperparameters, data hash and adapter artifacts. Compare against prompting and RAG baselines on accepted accuracy, calibration, safety, latency and total cost. Preserve source evidence outside weights.''',
'''Near-duplicate incidents appear in training and test, inflating accuracy. A fine-tuned model memorizes synthetic secret strings. Training loss falls while held-out performance declines, indicating overfitting.\n\nTest deduplication and grouped splits, evaluate rare classes and check that unsupported requests still abstain. Red-team the adapted model and do not infer security from a lower training loss.''',
'''Choose adaptation only if held-out gains justify operational complexity. Report what improved, what regressed and how the adapter can be disabled or rolled back.\n\nFor SDETs, the training pipeline itself needs contracts: split disjointness, label schema, finite losses, saved/reloaded equivalence and stable evaluation. Fine-tuning acceptance is based on independent task evidence.''',
"rank=4;input_dim=100;output_dim=80\nfull=input_dim*output_dim\nlora=rank*(input_dim+output_dim)\nassert lora<full",
"def lora_params(input_dim,output_dim,rank):\n    if min(input_dim,output_dim,rank)<=0:raise ValueError('dimensions')\n    return rank*(input_dim+output_dim)",
"assert lora_params(100,80,4)==720\ntry:lora_params(1,1,0);assert False\nexcept ValueError:pass","def lora_params(input_dim,output_dim,rank):\n    return 0",
('When is RAG generally better than fine-tuning?',['When the main need is current external knowledge','When authorization must be learned','When no evaluation data exists'],0,'Retrieval can update evidence without retraining weights and preserve source provenance.'),['Fine-tuning','LoRA','PEFT'])
lab(40,'tradeoffs','System design: trust boundaries, SLOs and release evidence',
'''AI system design begins with users, tasks, failure costs and measurable constraints. A support chatbot, failure triage service, test-generation platform and enterprise RAG system share components but have different permissions, latency and quality requirements. State what the system may decide autonomously and what requires review.\n\nA diagram is useful when it shows data flow, trust boundaries, state and failure paths. Boxes named LLM and database alone do not explain authorization, freshness, budgets or recovery.''',
'''Design the shared quality platform with authenticated API, typed domain services, retrieval, bounded agent tools, evaluation runner and report storage. Keep provider credentials server-side, tenant filters before retrieval and writes behind concrete approval.\n\nSpecify SLOs for latency, accepted accuracy and availability, plus hard invariants for privacy and tool effects. Plan cache scope, queue capacity, trace correlation, index updates and compatible rollback. Compare a deterministic baseline before adding autonomous orchestration.''',
'''A design scales generation workers but ignores vector-store filtering and evaluator capacity. Another claims zero hallucinations without an evidence/abstention mechanism. A human approval box has no definition of the exact action being approved.\n\nWalk through normal, ambiguous, malicious and outage requests. For each, identify state changes, budgets, authorization decisions and visible outcome. Check whether the same evidence can support a release and an incident investigation.''',
'''Produce a design memo with requirements, architecture, sequence flow, tradeoffs, evaluation plan, load plan and failure matrix. Demonstrate one complete request and one injected outage through the runnable platform.\n\nSDETs should ask what evidence would disprove the design's claims. Every major guarantee should connect to a test, metric, policy boundary or operational procedure that can actually be inspected.''',
"requirements={'tenant_isolation':True,'writes_require_approval':True,'max_steps':5,'deadline_seconds':15}\nassert requirements['max_steps']>0",
"def design_gate(evidence):\n    required={'authorization','evaluation','failure_recovery','latency','rollback'}\n    return required<=set(evidence) and all(bool(evidence[k]) for k in required)",
"assert design_gate(dict.fromkeys(['authorization','evaluation','failure_recovery','latency','rollback'],'artifact'))\nassert not design_gate({'latency':'artifact'})","def design_gate(evidence):\n    return False",
('What should a system guarantee connect to?',['Inspectable enforcement and test evidence','A box label alone','A model confidence statement'],0,'Architecture claims need concrete mechanisms and falsifiable validation.'),['SLO','Trust boundary','System design'])
